WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.925
How does injection moulding work?

2
00:00:03.475 --> 00:00:06.809
This is an injection moulding press, cut open.

3
00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:11.007
On the left the injection unit, on the right the mould.

4
00:00:11.557 --> 00:00:15.926
It makes a plastic part in seconds, the same every time.

5
00:00:16.826 --> 00:00:19.636
Pellets drop from the hopper into a heated barrel.

6
00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:23.425
Inside it a screw turns and carries them forward.

7
00:00:23.975 --> 00:00:26.007
The heat and the kneading melt them.

8
00:00:26.907 --> 00:00:29.775
Once there is enough melt in front of the screw,

9
00:00:29.975 --> 00:00:33.946
it shoots forward like a piston and injects the melt into the mould.

10
00:00:34.846 --> 00:00:37.818
The cavity in the mould has the shape of the part.

11
00:00:38.368 --> 00:00:42.106
Water in the steel cools the plastic until it is stiff.

12
00:00:43.006 --> 00:00:47.571
Then the mould opens and the part drops out, here onto a scale.

13
00:00:48.121 --> 00:00:51.941
How hot, how fast and how hard: it is all on one sheet.

14
00:00:53.141 --> 00:00:57.971
That is the setting sheet: the approved settings for one mould on one press.

15
00:00:58.521 --> 00:01:03.225
The setter, who sets up the press, starts every run from this sheet.

16
00:01:04.125 --> 00:01:09.617
Heater zones hold the melt temperature: for PP about 230 degrees.

17
00:01:10.167 --> 00:01:12.268
The mould is at 40 degrees.

18
00:01:13.468 --> 00:01:19.006
The screw pushes forward at the injection speed: here 80 millimetres per second.

19
00:01:19.556 --> 00:01:23.657
Filling fast keeps the plastic hot, so it flows far.

20
00:01:24.207 --> 00:01:26.181
Too fast gives burn marks.

21
00:01:27.081 --> 00:01:30.192
The flow front runs from the gate through the cavity.

22
00:01:30.742 --> 00:01:33.830
Against the cooler steel a thin skin freezes.

23
00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:41.475
From 60 to 12 millimetres is 48 millimetres on speed: 0.6 seconds of filling.

24
00:01:42.675 --> 00:01:45.494
At 12 millimetres lies the switchover point:

25
00:01:45.494 --> 00:01:48.248
the machine changes from speed to pressure.

26
00:01:49.148 --> 00:01:53.420
The cavity is then about 95 to 98 percent full.

27
00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.037
Switching too late gives flash.

28
00:01:57.237 --> 00:02:01.280
Now the holding pressure takes over, here 400 bar.

29
00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.883
The screw keeps pushing and packs in more plastic,

30
00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:07.614
because the part shrinks as it cools.

31
00:02:08.514 --> 00:02:13.181
The holding time lasts until the gate has frozen: here 4 seconds.

32
00:02:13.731 --> 00:02:15.890
After that nothing more can get in.

33
00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:23.364
Then 10 seconds of cooling, then opening and ejecting: a cycle of 18.6 seconds.

34
00:02:24.564 --> 00:02:28.732
During cooling the screw turns and moves back against the back pressure.

35
00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:30.698
That mixes the melt.

36
00:02:31.598 --> 00:02:35.606
On the screw tip sits the check ring, a small valve ring.

37
00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:41.441
During injection it closes, so the melt cannot flow back past the screw.

38
00:02:42.341 --> 00:02:44.918
In front of the screw a cushion remains.

39
00:02:45.468 --> 00:02:49.708
If it jumps from 2 to 8 millimetres, the ring is leaking.

40
00:02:50.908 --> 00:02:54.298
The process window is the area where every part is good.

41
00:02:54.848 --> 00:02:57.382
Sit in the middle, not on the edge.

42
00:02:57.932 --> 00:03:02.019
Then the process absorbs swings in material and temperature.

43
00:03:02.919 --> 00:03:07.969
Scientific moulding fills about 95 to 98 percent on speed,

44
00:03:08.169 --> 00:03:10.166
and packs the rest on pressure.

45
00:03:11.366 --> 00:03:20.900
The viscosity curve shows how thick the melt is at each speed: 1,280, 880, 825.

46
00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.738
It first falls steeply, then runs flat.

47
00:03:25.289 --> 00:03:26.763
Choose the speed there.

48
00:03:27.663 --> 00:03:32.587
The intensification ratio converts oil pressure into plastic pressure:

49
00:03:32.587 --> 00:03:33.642
often about 10.

50
00:03:34.542 --> 00:03:38.211
A pressure drop study measures the pressure per section: nozzle,

51
00:03:38.411 --> 00:03:40.303
runner, gate and part.

52
00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:44.464
Together 1,500 of the two thousand bar.

53
00:03:45.364 --> 00:03:49.523
That is 75 percent, under the 80 percent rule.

54
00:03:50.073 --> 00:03:54.032
So not pressure-limited: the press can still reach the set speed.

55
00:03:54.932 --> 00:03:59.309
The gate freeze study weighs parts at ever longer holding times.

56
00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:03.683
Once the weight levels off, the gate is shut.

57
00:04:04.883 --> 00:04:08.262
Validation proves the process in three steps,

58
00:04:08.462 --> 00:04:14.385
IQ/OQ/PQ: installation, the edges of the window, and production.

59
00:04:15.285 --> 00:04:19.534
After that the part weight watches the process: if the filling changes,

60
00:04:19.734 --> 00:04:21.162
the weight changes.

61
00:04:21.712 --> 00:04:28.426
In the PQ, 125 shots weighed 4.820 grams on average.

62
00:04:28.975 --> 00:04:35.677
Every hour you weigh a shot, within 4.802 to 4.838 grams.

63
00:04:36.577 --> 00:04:41.630
If the mould moves to another press, or a new material supplier comes in,

64
00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:43.641
revalidation follows.
